(module crux-oracle-matrix-drops-unwritable)
;; THE DENOMINATOR SHRUNK. Stage N keeps a pre-registered mutant that turned
;; out unwritable, nothing in the frozen surface instantiating the behaviour it
;; would perturb, and scores it `unwritable` rather than dropping it. This body
;; accepts any scored count at all, so a matrix that silently discarded every
;; row it could not write passes as complete.
;;
;; WHY THIS IS THE FAILURE THAT MATTERS. Dropping an unwritable row does not
;; change the numerator and does change the denominator, so it RAISES the kill
;; rate without killing anything. That is the same arithmetic as grading on the
;; questions you answered, and it fails in the flattering direction, which is
;; the direction nobody re-checks. The kill matrix is eliminative evidence and
;; its whole value is the honesty of what it ranged over; a rate over a
;; denominator that quietly excluded the hard cases is not weak evidence, it is
;; evidence for a different claim.
;;
;; The pre-registration in stage I is what makes the drop detectable at all:
;; the taxonomy is fixed in writing BEFORE the wave, so a row missing from the
;; matrix is missing against a committed list rather than against nothing.
;;
;; DISCRIMINATION, measured at v0.14.87 over this body:
;;
;;   [N13-DENOMINATOR]  -> REFUTED
;;   [N13-NO-DROP]      -> REFUTED
;;
;; BOTH refute, unlike crux-probe-polarity-inverted where one post survives.
;; The reason is the shape of the honest decision: matrix-complete?'s forward
;; clause is an EQUALITY rather than an implication, so a body that accepts
;; more is caught by the same post that catches one accepting less. An equality
;; post needs no converse partner to cover both directions, which is why this
;; module ships one crux where shape.llmll's row check needed two.
(def matrix-complete? [row-count: int
                       scored-count: int] -> bool

  (pre (and (>= row-count 0)
            (and (>= scored-count 0)
                 (<= scored-count row-count))))

  (post (= result (= scored-count row-count)))
  (post (=> (< scored-count row-count) (not result)))

  (>= scored-count 0))
